 the same number of points? Are there any students who don't fit the pattern?\n:::\n\n## Step 1: The basic scatterplot\n\n::: {.callout-tip collapse=\"true\"}\n## Reveal the scatterplot\n\n::: {#c916c2ab .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfig, ax = plt.subplots()\nsns.scatterplot(data=scores, x=\"hours_studied\", y=\"exam_score\",\n                color=\"#2a6f97\", s=40, alpha=0.8, ax=ax)\nax.set(xlabel=\"Hours studied\", ylabel=\"Exam score\",\n       title=\"More studying helps, but with diminishing returns\")\nplt.show()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](relationships_files/figure-html/cell-4-output-1.png){width=1060 height=661}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n[Three things are visible at once:]{.reveal-note}\n\n1. A clear **positive** relationship.\n2. It **curves**: the first few hours add a lot of points; hours 15–20 add very little.\n3. A small **cluster of students** who studied a lot but scored poorly.\n\n[That correlation coefficient, by the way, is r = 0\\.68. A single number can't tell you about the curve or the cluster.]{.reveal-note}\n:::\n\n## Step 2: Color by a third variable\n\nWhat explains the low-scoring cluster? There's one more column in the spreadsheet: whether the student attended the review session.\n\n::: {.callout-tip collapse=\"true\"}\n## Reveal the colored scatterplot\n\n::: {#69d796c9 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfig, ax = plt.subplots()\nsns.scatterplot(data=scores, x=\"hours_studied\", y=\"exam_score\", hue=\"attended_review\",\n                palette={\"Yes\": \"#2a6f97\", \"No\": \"#d1495b\"}, s=45, alpha=0.85, ax=ax)\n\n# Smooth curve for students who attended, to show the bend\nyes = scores[scores[\"attended_review\"] == \"Yes\"].sort_values(\"hours_studied\")\nfit = np.polyfit(np.log1p(yes[\"hours_studied\"]), yes[\"exam_score\"], 1)\ngrid = np.linspace(0, 20, 200)\nax.plot(grid, np.polyval(fit, np.log1p(grid)), color=\"#2a6f97\", lw=2, alpha=0.6)\n\nax.set(xlabel=\"Hours studied\", ylabel=\"Exam score\",\n       title=\"The outliers all skipped the review session\")\nax.legend(title=\"Attended review\")\nplt.show()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](relationships_files/figure-html/cell-5-output-1.png){width=1060 height=661}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n[Every student in the odd cluster skipped the review session. Finding that from the table means checking 150 rows across three columns; in the chart it's one glance at the red dots.]{.reveal-note}\n:::\n\n",
